# Realization — the debt against meaning

The tree says what the system **means**. The realization layer says whether the project
**fulfils** it. Two layers, never mixed: writing code does not change intent, and
rewriting a Meaning section does not change realization — it **invalidates** it.

## The one rule everything follows

**Assertions are stored. State is computed.**

Stored: someone claimed a node realized, with evidence, against fingerprints of the
wording it was claimed against — plus the human verdict, if any. Nothing else.

Computed: whether the claim still holds, why it stopped holding, what is blocked, and
what to work on next.

Consequence worth knowing: an inconsistent combination (a realized child under an
ancestor whose meaning moved) **cannot be stored**, so no rule has to forbid it.

## Fingerprints

| Fingerprint | Covers |
|---|---|
| `contracts` | the `contracts` list: id, text, `enforced_by`, reason — sorted by id |
| `meaning` | sections `## Refines`, `## Meaning`, `## Contracts`, `## Non-goals`, plus `parent` and `uses` |

Excluded on purpose: `slug`, `title`, `status`, `code_paths`, `test_paths`, `talks_to`,
open questions and YAML formatting. Renaming a node or moving its files is not a change
of what it commits to. Reordering contracts changes nothing either.

A typo fix *does* invalidate. That is the price of a gate that is arithmetic instead of a
language model, and `affirm` is the one-command answer to it.

## Derived states

| State | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `not_claimed` | nobody has ever asserted this node is fulfilled |
| `realized` | a claim exists, both fingerprints still match, every enforcer is reachable |
| `stale` | the wording moved since the claim — own, an ancestor's, or a `uses` target's contracts |
| `broken` | a contract points at an enforcer that no longer exists (renamed or deleted test) |
| `rejected` | the Human looked at the claim and said no |

Priority when several apply: `stale`, then `broken`, then `rejected`. Every reason is
printed, so nothing is lost to the priority.

Acceptance is a second, independent axis: `not_required`, `pending`, `approved`,
`rejected`. Approval is tied to the fingerprints it was given against — when the wording
moves, approval decays back to `pending`. Old approval never covers new text.

## Propagation — wording, never state

| From | To | Trigger |
|---|---|---|
| a node | its whole subtree | its `contracts` **or** `meaning` fingerprint changed |
| a node | its direct `uses` consumers, **one hop** | its `contracts` fingerprint changed |
| a node | anything, via `talks_to` | never |

A node that is merely unproven propagates **nothing**. An unclaimed ancestor does not
block its children — otherwise adoption would have to start at the root, the least
provable node in any tree, and one edit low in the stack would redden everything above it.
Project-wide compliance still requires that ancestor, so no guarantee is lost.

A `uses` edge is a dependency on a *definition*. If the target's prose improves, the
consumer's proof stands; if its commitment changes, it does not.

## Who may write what

| Action | Who | Refused for |
|---|---|---|
| `claim` | Coordinator, once every gate the level requires has passed — at `low` the Grader, above it the Adversary too | the **Coder** — nobody grades their own work |
| `affirm` | Human only | every agent role |
| `accept` / `reject` | Human only | every agent role |

`claim` refuses a node with an open question, and refuses a node whose contracts point at
a missing enforcer. These are recorded, not cryptographic, guarantees: anything written
in `by` is visible in the diff, which is exactly where the Adversary and the Human look.

A claim written before the review is one that nobody can withdraw: the fingerprints cover
the node's **text**, so repairing a blocker in a test never reddens it, and there is no
`unclaim` command. Where a review runs, the claim waits for its verdict — `REQUEST CHANGES`
leaves the node honestly unclaimed instead of claimed and contradicted.

## Commands

```bash
$TOOL realization status [--node iNNNN] [--json]   # derived state and reasons
$TOOL realization worklist [--json]                # what needs work, ancestors first
$TOOL realization summary                          # how much of the intent is realized
$TOOL realization claim iNNNN --evidence doc/runs/<run> --by Coordinator
$TOOL realization affirm iNNNN [--subtree] --by <human> --reason "..."
$TOOL realization accept iNNNN --by <human> [--note "..."] [--reject]
$TOOL realization check                            # R1-R7 over the layer; part of VERIFY
$TOOL realization prune                            # drop entries for nodes that left
```

`worklist` is the most general assignment there is: *bring every current node to
`realized`, and to `approved` where policy asks for it.* A node marked `blocked_by iNNNN`
waits for that ancestor — fixing the ancestor may change what the child needs.

## Policy

`doc/intent/_policy.yaml` — two profiles, both read-only for agents.

| `acceptance_profile` | Human sign-off required for |
|---|---|
| `none` | nothing |
| `standard` (default) | nodes with a contract `enforced_by: review` |
| `leaf` | plus every node owning `code_paths` |
| `strict` | every node |

| `evidence_profile` | a claim requires |
|---|---|
| `standard` (default) | a run directory containing `grader.md` |
| `relaxed` | reachable enforcers only; `evidence` may be `VERIFY.md` |

Changing this file is a hard trigger for complexity `high`, like `VERIFY.md`.

## How it fits the run

- **Slice** — `intent slice` prints the node's state and the reason it went stale. That
  is what the Coder needs: not only what the node means, but what moved.
- **Grader** — unchanged, and above `low` not sufficient alone. It proves the commands ran
  and passed, never that an enforcer reaches every place its sentence speaks about. That
  judgement is what a review produces, so where one runs, the claim waits for it too.
- **Scope guard** — `doc/intent/_realization.yaml` is always an allowed output and is
  **not** an intent change. It does not raise complexity and does not wake the Adversary.
- **Generated views** — `MAP.md` and `INDEX.json` deliberately carry no realization
  state. They are guarded for staleness, and a fast-moving column would make that guard
  fail on every claim.
- **`realization check`** verifies the layer is *consistent*, never that the project is
  *finished*. An unfinished project is the normal state; an inconsistent layer is a bug.

## Bootstrap

The layer starts as an empty file. Every node is `not_claimed`, which is the truth. No
heuristic marks anything done. A Human may then work through known-good parts — either
by running them through a normal run, or, in `relaxed`, by claiming against `VERIFY.md`.

## Never

- Never put realization state into a node's front matter — that is what this layer exists
  to prevent, and it would rewrite whole subtrees on every claim.
- Never let a language model decide whether something went stale. The gate is arithmetic
  over fingerprints.
- Never claim on behalf of the Human, and never sign a human decision with a role name.
